 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Clamps voltage transients, from lightning and similar surges, that would otherwise reach sensitive equipment. Named uses are I/O lines and VCC buses in telecom, computing, industrial and consumer equipment, plus ESD (IEC 61000-4-2) and EFT (IEC 61000-4-4) protection of data lines."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Response and leakage",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Turn-on from 0 V to VBR min typically takes under 1.0 ps. Typical IR is under 1 µA on parts with VBR min above 12 V. The junction is glass passivated and the package has low inductance and built-in strain relief."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulse rating",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The single-die parts are rated 400 W with a 10/1000 µs pulse at a repetition rate (duty cycle) of 0.01 %; the stacked-die parts are rated 600 W with a 10/1000 µs pulse. Pulse power falls with longer pulses (Fig. 2) and with initial junction temperature, to 60 % at 150 °C (Fig. 3)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Clamping",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Under a voltage transient the voltage across the part is held to a lower, flattened level while the surge current flows through the part (Fig. 1)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Failure mode",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Overstress beyond the voltage or current rating typically leaves the part shorted."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Assembly and materials",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Reflow solderable at 260 °C for 30 s; MSL 1 per J-STD-020. Matte tin, lead-free terminals (e3: tin finish, Pb-free second-level interconnect per J-STD-609A.01); halogen free and RoHS compliant; plastic package flammability rated V-0 per Underwriters Laboratories. Whisker testing follows JESD201A tables 4a and 4c."
  }
 ],
